      "id": 1,
      "text": "In 1878, the Louisville & Nashville railroad completed a spur line through Millbrook, opening the town to Montgomery and sparking year‑round residency.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/millbrook/"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Before white settlers arrived, the area that is now Millbrook was part of the Creek town of Coosawda.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/millbrook/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Robinson Springs, now a neighborhood of Millbrook, began as an early summer resort for wealthy Montgomery families in the early 1800s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Millbrook%2C_Alabama"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1852, Robinson Springs hosted Alabama’s first state fair, drawing visitors from across the state to its modest grounds.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Millbrook%2C_Alabama"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Millbrook’s boundaries include the former village of Robinson Springs, preserving its historic name within the modern city limits.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Millbrook%2C_Alabama"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Millbrook is part of the Montgomery Metropolitan Statistical Area, linking it economically and culturally to Alabama’s capital.",
